{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact head 5ff918e80e77e74972aab0bba483c02aa2fd55d7.\n\nThe implementation is mechanically careful and the concrete checkpoint case is reproduced. The branch and origin both name this exact clean head, directly based on main 04c4a267, and the commit carries the canonical af2299e3 Rests-On trailer. Gates are green: git diff --check, go vet ./..., go build ./..., focused go test ./internal/workroom ./cmd/gs -count=1, and full go test ./... -count=1. The full run includes cmd/gs at 168.953s, internal/kernel at 141.456s, internal/docset at 110.786s, internal/app at 53.990s, and every package passed.\n\nBLOCKER 1 — the widened authority is not reviewer-signed. reviewedPaths calls every effective unretired artifact that the implementer published at the exact head before the verdict a path the approval reviewed. The structured approval still names and rests on only one artifact. The other paths are therefore implementer-written claims that the reviewer did not sign or cite. Ordering them before the verdict fixes post-verdict minting but not pre-verdict widening: an implementer can publish a candidate at an unrelated path before requesting review, obtain an approval that cites only the legitimate primary artifact, and then use the approval to retire another actor pointer on the unrelated lineage. The existing published-after test misses exactly that case. This violates request af2299e3 condition 2, which requires actually reviewed artifacts without signer-written widening, and condition 4, which preserves refusal of unrelated artifacts. It also leaves stale secondary candidates outside the strict merge gate because reviewedPaths excludes retired artifacts but not stale ones, while only the named primary participates in approval staleness.\n\nRequired correction: make the reviewer sign the exact artifact set that widens receipt reach, for example as repeatable artifact citations or a canonical reviewed-set digest whose members are direct approval bases. The fold and CLI must derive reach only from that signed set and verify each member is effective, standing, at the exact approved head, owned by the implementer, and current under the strict merge rule. Add the missing negative mutation: an implementer artifact at the approved head and before the verdict, but absent from the reviewer-signed set, reaches nothing. The four checkpoint artifacts may then form the explicit positive case.\n\nBLOCKER 2 — the carried retirement exception is broader than a successor. mergeSuccessionPlan lends the plan of any cited live receipt to every effective record that cites it. An arbitrary later record can therefore cite a receipt and one of its planned predecessors and suppress staleness from that retirement even when the record is not an artifact published by that merge, is not at the merge head, and is not at a planned successor path. The signed plan bounds which retirement is hidden, but it does not bind the beneficiary. This changes the general provenance contract beyond the requested successor-on-its-own-predecessor case.\n\nRequired correction: keep the receipt own signed-plan exception, but carry it only to a genuine successor artifact bound to that receipt, merge head, and planned successor path. Ordinary descendants then inherit a current successor normally. Add a negative test showing that a non-successor record citing receipt plus planned predecessor still becomes stale, alongside the positive circular-successor test.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are Application profile and interpreter at internal/workroom, and CLI, MCP, skills, connectors, and UI surface at cmd/gs; Kernel, Git storage, nexus, and projection/query boundaries are unchanged. The head correctly updates architecture.md and merge.md in the same commit, but their claim that the derived paths are paths the approval reviewed is stronger than the signed record proves. The contract cannot be approved until the review set and successor beneficiary are explicit and fold-verifiable.\n\nSECURITY. Unresolved. Cross-author retirement is an authority boundary. The prior design bounded it by a reviewer-signed artifact; this head widens it with pre-verdict fields written by the authority seeker. The second blocker lets unrelated records suppress a causal freshness signal by borrowing a receipt. Both must fail closed before approval.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The cleanest contract is also the narrowest: one explicit reviewer-signed artifact set used by both fold and CLI, plus one predicate identifying actual merge successors. That removes the temporal inference, makes the mutation tests state the authority directly, and avoids the current documentation ambiguity. No further feature expansion is needed.\n\nI did not edit or merge either implementation head, did not retire any predecessor, and did not run gs merge.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:6a438f389b220fc8e73c051bc332a731c76dfb48","head":"5ff918e80e77e74972aab0bba483c02aa2fd55d7","verdict":"changes-requested"}}